# What to produce

`literatures`: four to eight entries, each a body of work that a title-and-abstract search for this question would retrieve although it does not answer it. Draw them from the research question and from the vocabulary of the benchmark abstracts: ask which other research traditions use the same words for a different question, the same setting for a different outcome, or the same outcome for a different cause. Each entry has:
- `key`: lower-case identifier with underscores
- `name`: the name researchers in that tradition would use for their own field
- `description`: two or three sentences saying what these studies examine, compare and measure, written the way their own abstracts would say it
- `differs_from_construct`: one sentence naming what the target studies require that this literature lacks, or what it adds that the target excludes
- `discriminating_cue`: the words or framing in a title or abstract that tell a record from this literature apart from an include
- `example_phrases`: three to five short phrases typical of abstracts in this literature

Stay with literatures a database search for this question would actually surface. Do not list topics that merely sound related, and do not invent fields.
